  "final_text": "All four steps are done. After the final edit, I read the rule back: it's enabled, checks every 1m, and its last run status is `ok`.\n\n1. **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" (id `90943e30-9a47-11e8-b64d-95841ca0b247`) points at the `kibana_sample_data_logs` index. Its time field is `timestamp`. It also has two other date fields, `@timestamp` and `utc_time`, but neither is the time field. Other fields:\n   - **Request details:** `request`, `url`, `response`, `bytes`, `extension`, `referer`, `agent`\n   - **Client and location:** `clientip`, `ip`, `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`\n   - **Machine:** `machine.os`, `machine.ram`, `memory`, `phpmemory`\n   - **Other:** `tags`, `message`, `host`, `hour_of_day`\n\n   Most text fields also have a `.keyword` version. The response code is stored as text, with an exact-match version in `response.keyword`.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** \"S4-378b50b8 Web logs\" (id `b2ce688c-da21-4971-af26-a494bf3bb0ec`) has exactly two panels, both using the saved data view:\n   - a line chart of request count over time on `timestamp`\n   - a bar chart of request count for the top 10 values of `response.keyword`\n\n3. **Alert rule:** \"S4-378b50b8 5xx errors\" (id `b320f3c8-0656-4194-9925-0e3cd5bb2a80`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute and fires when the count of requests with a response code from 500 to 599 in the last 5 minutes is above 10. It was created enabled.\n   - I turned off the default setting that skips documents already matched in the previous run, so each check counts every server error in the full 5-minute window.\n   - Because the response code is text, the 500\u2013599 range compares strings, not numbers. That's exact for three-digit codes, which is what this data contains.\n\n4. **Panel edit:** The response-code panel is now titled \"Responses by code\", and the other panel is unchanged.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
